WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.629
This paper can simulate really sophisticated
virtual material models.

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:11.980
Here, billions and billions of numbers describe
the reflectance properties of these materials.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.410
But, material properties are not the only
thing that is important in a virtual world.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.769
Textures are important too.

00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:25.320
And with textures, I have good news and bad
news for you.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.099
Good news: they look fantastic, so much so
that you can take a photograph from the real

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:34.660
world and add it to your video games.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.300
Bad news: they take a ton of data.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:43.970
Gigabytes and gigabytes of data that is often
too much for real-time applications and handheld

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.050
devices.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:51.900
And sometimes, it is too much even for big
honking machines and video games.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.900
Why?

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.120
Well, you see in a video game or virtual world,
when we get closer to an object, we need to

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.010
see more detail.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:07.280
Imagine going closer to an open book, and
suddenly, a blurry mess emerges.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.400
What instead should happen is that we should
be able to even read that book.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.680
So, what does that require?

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.890
Storing more detail means, yes, you’ve guessed
right, Fellow Scholars, that means storing

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.140
even more data.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.310
So, what is the solution?

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:31.180
Well, if too much data is the problem, is,
of course, the solution is compression.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.180
Compression means making things smaller.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.180
More compact.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:40.610
We are in luck, because these shiny neural
networks are excellent at compression.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:45.549
We just saw how well they have done this job
with the material models here.

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:49.710
They are also excellent at compressing simulation
data.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:55.299
So, scientists at NVIDIA said, let’s do
exactly that here too.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:59.539
And now, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,
and look.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:03.530
This is the previous technique, and this is
the new one.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:10.040
For approximately the same amount of data
storage, this can provide so much better quality.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.180
Wow.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.540
That is excellent.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.540
But wait, is it excellent enough for real-time
applications?

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:25.099
I mean, how many seconds does it take to then
decompress this and use it in our games?

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:30.140
Dear Fellow Scholars, and this is where I
fell off the chair when reading this paper.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.860
It is not seconds.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.019
A thousand times less.

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:35.019
Yes.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:37.590
Just approximately one millisecond.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.570
That is all it takes to decompress all this
information.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.390
And you are going to love this one.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.610
Do you see this flickering?

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.920
This is the process of compression by the
neural network.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.349
It means that it is at work now.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:58.459
The flickering arises as the technique is
trying a lot of different, but similar ways

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:00.430
to compress it down.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.300
Okay, so is this good?

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.670
Is it doing better over time or worse?

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.459
Well, look here.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:14.230
Here you see the signal to noise ratio, this
is the quality of the reconstruction, the

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.480
higher the better.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.920
And, oh my, I love this.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:25.940
Over time, this is increasing steadily, and
don’t forget, the decibel scale is logarithmic,

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:32.180
which means a lot of things, but among those
lot of things, it means that even a small

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.569
numerical difference means a big difference
in quality.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:39.750
So, how long does this take?

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:40.750
Days?

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:41.750
Hours?

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.349
Ah, wait.

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:44.659
We are done.

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:48.790
The whole process took less than a minute
per texture.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.129
That is excellent.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:56.329
So, with that, if we have this beautiful inkwell
in a video game, with previous technique,

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:02.319
when going closer to it, we saw this, and
now, we see this.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:05.180
And all this in real time.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.270
That is incredible.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:12.099
Because of the papers, next level games and
virtual worlds are coming.

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:15.430
But wait, one more final question.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:21.010
This looks good, but what would be the proper,
real solution?

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:25.910
How much information did we lose during this
compression?

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:27.820
And the answer is.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.360
Almost nothing.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.410
Wow.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:37.070
Note that this technique is specifically designed
for storing textures, and thus, it also includes

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:42.669
compressing different versions of the same
image together that we call mip levels and

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:44.160
some more magic.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.840
And, here is a bombshell result.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.680
The previous technique could do this, the
new technique can do this.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.660
Wow, that book is readable even if we magnify
it a great deal.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.750
And, my goodness.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:01.810
Look at that!

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:09.340
It looks nearly the same as the true solution,
and takes about 45 times less data.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.480
Phew!

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.039
And do not worry for a second, a bunch of
other techniques are also tested against in

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:17.039
the paper.

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:19.740
Now, not even this one is perfect.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:26.520
For very low bitrates, when we are really
short on memory, not so good.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.310
Still better than previous techniques, but
not ideal.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:35.730
But that is an incredible jump in quality
in just one paper.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:36.730
Loving it.

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:59.490
What a time to be alive!

